Approval reuse is now bounded by age, not by a nonce that never expired anything
=================================================================================
Date : 2026-08-02
Mode : production entry point (`resolve_incident_scope`) + the repo's own probe
Gate : make check 1611 -> 1614 (+3)

Decision  : DECISIONS.md D42 — 결정 6, option C (perishable, not single-use)
Brief     : docs/plans/2026-08-02-nonce-replay-scope.md


What was wrong (measured 2026-08-02, before the change)
--------------------------------------------------------
`AttestedApproval.nonce` was documented "One-time-use marker; the broker rejects a
replayed nonce". `TokenBroker.mint` did reject one — against `self._spent`, an
INSTANCE attribute — and the only production caller builds a fresh broker per call:

    동일한 attested 레코드를 프로덕션 경로로 3회 제출:
      시도 1: MINTED  tenant=acme
      시도 2: MINTED  tenant=acme
      시도 3: MINTED  tenant=acme

`test_nonce_replay_is_refused` passed throughout, because it held ONE broker and
called `mint` twice — the test supplied the only condition under which the guard
worked.

Real one-time-use was NOT the fix: `aws/executor.py` resolves the scope for the
same incident on both the runbook path (:172) and the action path (:253), and a
retried Step Function does it again. Enforcing single-use would refuse legitimate
callers. So the question was never "persist the set", it was "what is one use".


What shipped: option C
------------------------
`issued_at` (epoch seconds, stamped at the moment authorization exists) is added to
the SIGNED payload, and the broker refuses anything older than its TTL.

  TTL default 900s (`PLATFORM_APPROVAL_TTL_SECONDS`). Not a round number picked
  for comfort: `attest_decision` signs at the instant authorization exists (AUTO =
  the Guardian policy decision, APPROVE = the human resolving the parked approval),
  and the executor consumes it in the same flow. There is no human wait inside that
  window BY DESIGN, so what must fit is machine time — dispatch, SFN retries, queue
  latency. 15 minutes is generous for that and short enough that a leaked record is
  not a standing credential.


Measured after, through `resolve_incident_scope` (not a hand-held broker)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ① fresh approval, submitted 3x (the executor's real pattern)
       시도 1: MINTED   시도 2: MINTED   시도 3: MINTED
     Reuse inside the TTL still works. This is the honest limit of option C and it
     is asserted, not left to be discovered.

  ② past the TTL
       refused: approval 'APR-old' is 960s old, past the 900s TTL —
                re-authorize rather than reusing it

  ③ expired record with only the timestamp freshened, signature untouched
       refused: approval 'APR-old' failed attestation — refusing to mint
     `issued_at` is inside the signed payload, so the stamp cannot be pushed
     forward without the key.

  ④ stamped 24h into the future to make it effectively immortal
       refused: approval 'APR-fut' is stamped 86400s in the future — refusing
                (clock skew, or a record built to outlive its TTL)

  ⑤ the repo's own probe, end to end
       scripts/probe_scope_reachability.py -> resolve_incident_scope minted,
       guard_scoped_action -> PERMITTED, verdict "scope reachable: True"


A branch that would have been unreachable, caught by measuring
----------------------------------------------------------------
The first version refused `issued_at == 0` with the comment "signed by a pre-TTL
version". Measured — that scenario never reaches the age check:

    구버전 서명 레코드의 새 payload : {"approval_id":"A","env":"dev","issued_at":0,...}
    구버전 payload                 : {"approval_id":"A","env":"dev","nonce":"n",...}
    verify() 통과?  False  → 나이 검사에 도달하지 못한다

`issued_at` is in the payload, so a record signed by the old build fails `verify()`
first and reported "failed attestation" — true, and during a rolling deploy it is
also the normal case, so it would send an operator hunting a forgery.

Fixed both halves: `_signed_by_a_pre_ttl_version` recomputes the OLD payload shape
purely to NAME the failure (it never grants — falsified below), and the zero branch
is re-documented as what it actually catches, an explicitly unstamped signature.


Falsification (each mechanism separately red)
-----------------------------------------------
  remove the age check entirely          -> 3 failed  (TTL, config, zero-stamp)
  drop issued_at from the signed payload -> 2 failed  (skew naming, forge-forward)
  turn the skew diagnosis into accept    -> 1 failed  ("naming it must never turn
                                                       into accepting it")


What this does NOT claim
--------------------------
Reuse within the TTL is possible, and the docstrings, the plan doc and
`tests/test_scope_replay_reachability.py` all say so in those words. Tenant
boundary was never the exposure: the signature covers the tenant, so a replayed
record re-mints the SAME scope and cannot cross — asserted. What the TTL buys is
that an approval stops being a credential; true one-time-use, keyed per action and
persisted, remains open as option B and belongs with Phase 4 (it needs state in all
three executor adapters).


Reproduce
-----------
  export PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL_DIR=... PLATFORM_APPROVAL_SIGNING_KEY=...
  python scripts/probe_scope_reachability.py
  python -m pytest tests/test_scope_replay_reachability.py -v
